commit 07674e916543b863485e19cbda8f58f44da88709
Author: Craig Xu Chen <craigxchen@gmail.com>
Date:   Thu Jun 4 22:41:42 2026 -0400

    Add conservative grain→water dissolution pass
    
    Phase 1.5 U5: wet grains release two-pool solute into overlapping water's
    concentration `c`, conserving the solute inventory exactly without atomics —
    the Phase-1.4 wetting transfer pattern applied to chem.
    
    - extraction.wgsl: diss_count (per-particle eligible-opposite count + per-grain
      aggregate relative-velocity flux), dissolve_grain, dissolve_water. Both
      transfer passes read a frozen chem snapshot + frozen counts and write disjoint
      slots of live chem, so grain-loss == water-gain to float tolerance with no
      read/write race. The two-sided cap
      take = min(release/N_w, max(c_sat−c,0)·(f_w·V_w)/N_g) unifies conservation,
      the (1−c/c_sat) driving force (water-headroom cap), and the f_w coupling.
      Passes read `pos` (post-wetting moisture; pos.xyz==pred.xyz post-finalize),
      keeping both transfer passes at exactly 8 storage buffers.
    - mod.rs: diss_neighbors scratch buffer (b21), three pipelines + lean bind
      groups, chem→chem_frozen snapshot + substep block after wetting gated
      `mixed && extract_rate>0` (rebuilds its own grid). write_chem_for_test /
      write_moisture_for_test helpers.
    - common.wgsl: diss_neighbors binding (b21).
    - tests/xpbd_extraction.rs: solute-conservation gate (dissolution isolated,
      constant f_w), opt-in gate, dry-grain gate, c_sat cap, saturated-neighbor,
      depleted-grain guard, flux monotonicity, CPU/GPU parity.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>

